WEBVTT

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:02.820
Help all kids learn and grow with PBS Kids.

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.380
Thank you for supporting your PBS station.

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:44.570
See you next time.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.850
I love fiddling with pipe cleaners.

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:12.970
You can be really creative with them.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:17.360
They're easy to use, and with just a few twists and turns,

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:21.450
you can make just about anything.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.380
Pretty good, huh?

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:29.980
Pipe cleaners are soft and fuzz.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.260
on the outside but the reason they're so flexible is that on the inside they're

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:46.460
made of wire wire bends and curls but it's also amazingly strong which is why

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.960
so many things we use every day are made of wire

00:02:54.510 --> 00:02:56.830
As you can see, wire is almost everywhere.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:59.940
People all over the world find ways to use it.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:06.100
In this book, a young African boy builds something out of wire that's very clever.

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.600
It's called Galimoto.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:29.260
Galimoto by Karen Lynn William, illustrated by Catherine Stock.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:35.080
Read by Hottie Winston.

00:03:41.430 --> 00:03:48.310
Conde opened an old shoebox and inside were his things.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:53.680
There was a knife Conde had made from a tin can

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:59.480
and a dancing man he had made from dried corn stalks.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:05.700
In Kondi's box, there were also some scraps of wire.

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.340
He had been saving for something special.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:17.330
I shall make a galimoto, Kondi told his brother, Ufulu.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:25.260
Ufulu, la, a boy with only seven years cannot make such a toy.

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:27.560
You don't have enough wire.

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:29.980
I will get...

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:34.410
enough wire. Kondi took

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:36.670
his knife and went to the home

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:38.350
of his friend Gift.

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.430
Want your wires?

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:44.090
Kondi told Gift. I'll give you my

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:46.980
knife for them. Why do

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.960
you want the wires? Gift asked.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.430
I want to make a gallimoto.

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:54.840
Kondi answered. Gift took

00:04:54.840 --> 00:04:56.880
a handful of wires from his

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:58.740
box of things and gave them to

00:04:58.740 --> 00:04:59.420
Kondi.

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:03.670
That's not enough wire for a galimoto, Gif told his friend.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.160
I will find enough wire.

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:15.140
Kondi put his wires into an old bag and took them to the shop of his uncle.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:22.970
If you want the wires you have on your old packing boxes, they would make a fine galimoto.

00:05:23.590 --> 00:05:28.090
So, you want to make a galimoto?

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:29.970
This son...

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:42.060
of my brother. He's a clever boy. Take the wires. Condi could hear the grinders at the

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:52.990
flour meal. Many women with babies tied on their backs waited in the hut. Condi squeezed

00:05:52.990 --> 00:05:59.840
through the crowd. The women began yelling angrily. Wait, you cannot go there. Stop him.

00:06:01.340 --> 00:06:05.680
I have no maize to grind. I only want wires for a girl in moto.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:12.260
The miller shrugged. There are some wires out back. Those ain't go.

00:06:16.730 --> 00:06:25.610
The mill, Kondi found some very thin wires wrapped in red and yellow and green plastic.

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.260
And scraped the coating off, Kondi thought.

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:37.820
Poundy passed some young children playing on an hill.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:43.090
One was Moondy, the small sister of his friend Gift.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:51.120
She had a fine long piece of wire, which she stuck into the dirt.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:56.460
Little friend, you cannot catch an ant that way.

00:06:56.960 --> 00:06:59.980
With some water and a stick, we will...

00:07:00.320 --> 00:07:09.510
them condi poured water around one of the holes now they will think the rains have begun condi

00:07:09.510 --> 00:07:20.940
explained then he stuck a stick down the hole gently he pulled it out and the end was a large

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:29.790
my stick is better than a wire for catching ants you may have this

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.440
And I'll take the wire.

00:07:35.040 --> 00:07:40.660
Condi took his wires to the shade of the red flame trees in his village.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.060
Condi sorted his wires.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.590
They were thick pieces and thin pieces.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.580
Some were long and some were short.

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:56.290
Condi banged the bent and twisted ones with a stone to straighten them.

00:07:57.480 --> 00:07:59.970
These thick wires made the...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:07.500
frame he wrapped the very thinnest wires at the joints to hold the galley motor

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:11.900
together my galley motor will be a pickup truck

00:08:11.900 --> 00:08:18.380
kondi decided his brothers and sisters admired his work

00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:29.980
oh you found enough wire ufulu said yeah kondi agreed the moon be bright for us to play

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:37.840
and sing tonight. Condi could hear his friends singing in the distance, calling him to play.

00:08:39.390 --> 00:08:49.940
He carefully guided his gallimotor over the dusty path. Someone cheered. One by one,

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:53.720
Condi's friends formed a line behind him.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.660
They began to churn.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:14.440
And he saw the shadow of his galimoto

00:09:14.440 --> 00:09:18.540
cast by the moonlight racing alongside.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:23.600
It's a fine galimoto, he thought proudly.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:29.840
Tomorrow, I shall make my galimoto into...

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:35.610
Bandula, or plane, or helicopter.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:53.470
Gandhi would have a great time with this piece of wire, even though it is kind of wild.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:58.550
Seems to have a mind of its own, and it moves all over the place.

00:10:03.390 --> 00:10:07.930
There's an artist in Santa Fe, New Mexico, who's an expert at Taming Wire,

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.680
and she really knows how to whip it in shape.

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:54.080
My name is Eleanor McKay and I make sculptures out of wire.

00:10:55.820 --> 00:10:59.840
I never ever thought that I would be making wire.

00:11:00.580 --> 00:11:08.650
I just picked up a piece of wire and started bending it.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:15.940
I use very simple tools, wire cutters, and needle nose pliers, and my hands.

00:11:19.460 --> 00:11:21.800
It's like drawing, drawing in space.

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:49.440
The nature of wire is like a spring.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:53.850
It's kind of an ornery medium.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:59.340
I sometimes feel like I'm really wrestling with it,

00:11:59.550 --> 00:11:59.970
turning it.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:06.900
and bending it and crimping it and having to crimp it really hard and I have to tame

00:12:07.050 --> 00:12:12.470
the wire and it controls me there's it there becomes a relationship between me

00:12:12.470 --> 00:12:25.920
and the wire this piece I'm starting is going to be an armadillo and it's going

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.060
to be three-dimensional

00:12:30.700 --> 00:12:33.720
I like to use one piece of wire when I make my sculptures.

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:39.430
I have to bend it and tie it and make knots in it.

00:12:39.530 --> 00:12:43.200
And while I'm doing that, it turns the wire all around

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:44.880
and all upside down and all backwards.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:48.690
And I think that there's no way this is going to come out

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:52.030
and look like what I want it to look like.

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:54.490
And then I just keep on going and I keep making it.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:56.440
And somehow it does.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:57.400
It just comes out.

00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:08.980
When I'm done with bending and twisting and shaping them,

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:11.760
they're still just plain steel, gray,

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:15.420
and I think that they need more colors, so I like to paint them.

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:22.750
The colors are very bright, very rich.

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:29.960
They make the sculpture stand out.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:35.380
more they become alive and fun

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:25.890
and to wrestling with a wire, when I'm finally done,

00:14:26.780 --> 00:14:29.870
I'm always very happy with it, whether it's a rooster.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.900
or an armadillo, or a person, or even just a leaf.

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:56.260
This is remarkable.

00:14:56.540 --> 00:14:59.300
I feel like I'm sailing up the side of a mountain.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:01.320
And in a way, I am.

00:15:01.960 --> 00:15:05.820
I'm traveling on the Sandia Peak Tramway in Albuquerque, New Mexico.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:12.210
And the car that I'm riding in is hanging on a wire.

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:19.460
There are two track wires for the car to ride on.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:25.510
Between the track wires, there's a haul cable, which pulls the car up and down.

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.520
This is what the haul cable looks like, and this is the inside.

00:15:35.060 --> 00:15:40.440
See, it's really just a bunch of smaller wires, each one no bigger than the wire Ellie or Condi might have used.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:49.840
But when these strands are twisted together, they form a cable strong enough to haul this tramway car up and down the mountain.

00:17:08.920 --> 00:17:15.990
For most of us, riding in a tram car like this, putting so much trust in a piece of wire is

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:17.460
a once-in-a-lifetime experience.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:22.500
But for certain people, trusting in a wire is something they do every day.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:29.100
I guess you could say they put their lives right on the line.

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:34.540
Ladies and gentlemen, and children of all ages,

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:37.740
Raidling Brothers and Barnum and Bailey Circus

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:42.320
presents the first family of High Wire, the Kiros!

00:17:50.530 --> 00:17:52.230
My name is Ángel Kiros,

00:17:52.470 --> 00:17:55.330
and my family and I walk the High Wire.

00:18:03.930 --> 00:18:06.350
There is no net to catch you if you fall.

00:18:06.570 --> 00:18:08.930
So you must concentrate completely

00:18:08.930 --> 00:18:11.280
and always keep your eye on the wire.

00:18:18.800 --> 00:18:22.580
When you walk the wire, your life depends on the wire.

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:26.350
There is nothing else beneath your feet.

00:18:27.300 --> 00:18:29.240
The wire is all you have.

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:38.100
The sword fight is very hard to do.

00:18:38.440 --> 00:18:43.070
You must watch the tip of the sword as well as keep your eye on the wire.

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:48.060
You know how high up you are.

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:50.770
And sometimes you feel afraid.

00:18:51.520 --> 00:18:55.600
But you know if you work well, the wire will not let you down.

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:07.250
The wire is about the width of your thumb, and it feels hard, like a pipe beneath your feet.

00:19:11.540 --> 00:19:17.010
It is tightly strung, but it bounces a little, so you must work hard to keep your balance.

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:28.700
Concentration is crucial on the wire, and every time you go up there, you must give 100%.

00:19:29.490 --> 00:19:29.970
That...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:32.060
That can be hard when you hear the audience shout.

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:45.410
Many high-wire artists perform with their families because you must practice so much

00:19:45.410 --> 00:19:49.260
and trust each other totally, working with families best.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:03.520
many hours a day, but even so, we can make a mistake.

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:09.430
We learn how to catch ourselves and swing on the wire,

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:12.370
and repeat the performance right the next time.

00:20:32.460 --> 00:20:37.820
Your life depends on your partners, so you must trust them completely.

00:20:38.900 --> 00:20:43.450
You must also believe in yourself, and you must trust the wire.

00:21:34.860 --> 00:21:36.880
It's spectacular up here.

00:21:37.100 --> 00:21:38.920
It looks like we're on top of the world.

00:21:39.160 --> 00:21:46.920
It must have been incredibly difficult to build this tramway,

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:48.760
but it sure was worth it.

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:52.050
Lucky for us, not all projects are quite so difficult.

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:54.250
So, if you like to make things,

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:57.030
here are some books with simpler ideas,

00:21:57.150 --> 00:21:59.870
but you don't have to take my word for it.

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:28.360
Hi, Jessica Hellings. Look at this. That's the title of the book I just read. It gives you three fun ideas on how to make things. Do you know how to make a dancing frog? Find a piece of green paper, eight paper fasteners, a scissor, and a hole puncher. It's pretty easy to make. And the frog really dances.

00:22:30.000 --> 00:22:31.280
Pictures are really good.

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:34.310
They show you exactly what to do.

00:22:35.400 --> 00:22:37.820
The noisemaker is a funny story.

00:22:38.980 --> 00:22:41.600
A little boy makes his own noisemaker.

00:22:42.320 --> 00:22:45.400
The noises you can make will be really funny.

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:46.540
Try it.

00:22:47.610 --> 00:22:51.260
Time's up for this story, and time's up for me, too.

00:22:51.980 --> 00:22:54.680
But remember this book the next time

00:22:54.680 --> 00:22:56.500
you're looking for something to do.

00:22:57.190 --> 00:22:59.720
Hi, I'm Chris Giles.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:04.700
And if you like to make things and read a great book too, then The Little Pigs Puppet

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:05.660
Book is for you.

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:10.790
This story is about three brothers, Charles, Ralph, and Bertram.

00:23:11.500 --> 00:23:14.020
It's a rainy day and they don't know what to do.

00:23:14.780 --> 00:23:16.720
They decide to put on a puppet show.

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:19.500
First, they have to make puppets.

00:23:20.180 --> 00:23:21.900
Each one has a different idea.

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:29.980
They also have to write the script, make the stage, paint the scenery, and of course they

00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:31.560
They won't forget the refreshments.

00:23:32.560 --> 00:23:37.330
It's a lot of work to put on a puppet show, but the brothers really enjoyed it.

00:23:38.220 --> 00:23:40.060
I think it would be a lot of fun.

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:46.270
So the next time you're bored and don't know what to do, pop into your bookstore and pick

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:47.990
up The Little Pig's Puppet Book.

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:51.420
Hi, I'm Caroline Chang.

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:56.280
If you're feeling like you have nothing to do, then read my first activity book.

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:58.720
It's a great way to keep you busy.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:03.860
This book has a lot of different activities to try,

00:24:04.940 --> 00:24:07.100
like making jewelry from pasta.

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:10.830
What about turning eggs into faces?

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:16.030
You can try your hand at making puppets out of envelopes

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:19.050
and lanterns out of melons.

00:24:19.860 --> 00:24:22.780
Each activity has easy steps to follow.

00:24:23.980 --> 00:24:26.880
Color photographs show you exactly what to do.

00:24:28.030 --> 00:24:29.670
My favorite activity was potato.

00:24:30.030 --> 00:24:33.210
Can you believe I made this picture with a potato?

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:36.240
Find out how it's done in this book.

00:24:44.880 --> 00:24:49.320
There's something so satisfying about crafting things by hand.

00:24:49.640 --> 00:24:54.160
And no matter what you make, half the fun is choosing your material.

00:24:55.210 --> 00:24:58.100
So for today, I'll stick to wire.

00:25:03.520 --> 00:25:04.280
See you next time.

00:25:32.556 --> 00:25:34.556
Thanks for watching!

